  "body": "A terminal output return commutes through the workspace unload, so it and its inverse can be deleted from PR1537's fixed dirty echo. With intermediate cuts, the completed word uses **2,222 ADD/SCALE operations at prefix row-L1 cap 4,096** (actual peak 3,958). The pure canonical cut optimum at that cap costs 2,234. All source transfers, fixed children, inverses, remaining returns and integer-grid scaling are included.\n\n**Status: scoped recursive-interface construction and exact finite optimization; no new kappa.** Only `research/unipotent-precision-cuts-seed55/` is added.\n\n## What this adds\n\n- The disjoint-register return cancellation is proved and emitted. Alone it reduces the original uncut 2,234-operation word to 2,208 without increasing its peak.\n- A complete 1,431-interval graph certifies the extended **35-point cost/cap frontier** over all `2^52` contiguous partitions. Its unrestricted minimum is 2,206 operations; this is an optimum only in the declared fixed-child, fixed-order, binary-return class.\n- The seed-selected cap-256 target fails: no saving below cap 788. This remains true after extending the class with the cancellation.\n- A complete recursive invoice is implemented; the nested fixture costs 4,720 versus 4,960. Local precision caps are explicitly not assumed to compose.\n\nConcurrent **PR1552** (`4db7213c90288b1eb83983e08c5ef401d19b7844`) already supplies the 52+1 reset and the entire **31-point pure canonical frontier**. All 31 cost/cap pairs agree with this package's control. Those results are corroboration, not a new priority claim. The distinct result here is the **cancelled return pair and the 35-point extended frontier**, including the lower common-cap invoice. No PR1552 code is imported.\n\n## Task, sources and scope\n\nOS-random assignment seed **55**, recursive interfaces; the `persistent-boundary` card is refined to the exact PR1537 fixture. Research base `64039f1bd3b6a523b96083fd858ad040a75e3030`; predecessor PR1537 `407f00df6ca0bf86b455b5640f18ec2f62ee77b9`. Its original model, README and receipt are retained unchanged with SHA256/Git-blob pins. The patch applies cleanly to refreshed main `c5108f0f68d482e5712a8e26a5978d47762dfc16`.\n\nThe fixed-child restriction and unobserved midpoint are essential. No actual selected supplier with this indivisible interface is supplied. Physical routing, hosting, width-dependent bit costs, rounded/tape admission and contracting input-size transfer remain open. The complete scalar invoice is not a measured speedup. Selected PR1094 and `upstream/` are unchanged.\n\n[Claim](https://github.com/CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds/discussions/1542). Task, exact overlap coverage and source pins are included. The final helper refresh covered 715 open PRs and 217 discussion records with complete pagination and no warnings; source diffs and every closed PR were not searched.\n\n## Reproduce and validation\n\n```sh\npython3.12 -B scripts/research_package.py --package research/unipotent-precision-cuts-seed55\n```\n\nExpected: `PASS_PRECISION_CAPPED_UNIPOTENT_CUTS`, four passing artifact tests, then `PASS_REGISTERED_RESEARCH_PACKAGE ... outcome=completed`.\n\nPassing local evidence: registered Python 3.12.13 replay; optimized Python 3.14.6 replay; 216 separately derived boundary states; 132 directed Fraction basis columns and six attaining sign inputs; 4,858 small partition replays with 732 cap decisions; full nested maps; nine mathematical controls; real source/receipt corruptions, dual tampering and read-only relocation. Selected-record integrity, package provenance and clean-patch checks pass. An initial tuple/list receipt-normalization failure was repaired without changing the mathematical receipt; validation records the failure and successful rerun.\n\nFull selected cold replay, hosted CI, independent human review and proof-assistant verification are not claimed.\n\n## Credit and open questions\n\nCredit Sagar Thakrar's PR1537 for the fixed contract, binary returns, emitter and reset-cut question; Ross Richardson's PR1287/889 and Sagar's PR1227 for the frame/echo lineage; and the concurrent Hanson Sun PR1552 for canonical-cut corroboration. Prepared by **Hanson Sun (@bbMisk), with substantial OpenAI Codex assistance** in mathematics, code, checks and prose. Apache-2.0. Emulate's style pass was unavailable because only 18 word credits remained; this was disclosed and prose was checked directly against evidence.\n\nThe README's next questions concern one real indivisible caller and a globally capped nested caller; each names its first step and blocker.\n",
